<comment datatype='string'>PaaZ (oxepin-CoA hydrolase/3-oxo-5,6-dehydrosuberyl-CoA semialdehyde dehydrogenase) is a two-domain enzyme that catalyzes the two-step conversion of &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD0-2363&quot;&gt;2-oxepin-2(3H)-ylideneacetyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD0-2364&quot;&gt;3-oxo-5,6-didehydrosuberyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; in phenylacetate catabolism.  The PaaZ protein consists of an N-terminal NADP-dependent aldehyde dehydrogenase domain and a C-terminal MaoC-like (&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt;)-specific enoyl-CoA hydratase domain [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20660314&quot;&gt;Teufel10&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21296885&quot;&gt;Teufel11&lt;/a&gt;].  The specific activity of the enzyme for the overall reaction is ~20 &amp;micro;M min&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; mg&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; protein [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/20660314&quot;&gt;Teufel10&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
3-oxo-5,6-dehydrosuberyl-CoA semialdehyde is a labile intermediate and can undergo spontaneous conversion to the dead-end product &lt;a href=&quot;/compound?orgid=META&amp;id=CPD0-2558&quot;&gt;2-hydroxycyclohepta-1,4,6-triene-1-carboxyl-CoA&lt;/a&gt; through a Knoevenagel-type condensation [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21296885&quot;&gt;Teufel11&lt;/a&gt;].  Avoiding this fate, 3-oxo-5,6-dehydrosuberyl-CoA is transferred between the two PaaZ active sites by electrostatic pivoting of the CoA moiety.  The positively charged residues K69, R613 and K636 appear to play a role in channeling [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/31511507&quot;&gt;Sathyanarayanan19&lt;/a&gt;]
&lt;p&gt;
Cryo-EM structures of PaaZ show a tri-lobed structure consisting of three domain-swapped homodimers in which the C-terminal hydratase domains form the central core [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/31511507&quot;&gt;Sathyanarayanan19&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
PaaZ exhibits enoyl-CoA hydratase activity utilizing crotonyl-CoA as a substrate.  In a &lt;i&gt;fadB&lt;/i&gt; mutant, PaaZ produces (R)-3-hydroxyacyl-CoA for polyhydroxyalkanoate biosynthesis by exogenous &lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas&lt;/i&gt; medium-chain-length polyhydroxyalkanoate synthase [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/12949091&quot;&gt;Park03&lt;/a&gt;]. 
&lt;p&gt;
A &lt;i&gt;paaZ&lt;/i&gt; mutant exhibits a defect in utilization of phenylacetate as a source of carbon [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/12846838&quot;&gt;Ismail03&lt;/a&gt;].  
&lt;p&gt;
PaaZ: &quot;&lt;b&gt;p&lt;/b&gt;henyl&lt;b&gt;a&lt;/b&gt;cetic &lt;b&gt;a&lt;/b&gt;cid degradation&quot; [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/9748275&quot;&gt;Ferrandez98&lt;/a&gt;]
&lt;p&gt;
</comment>
